<article class="post">
<header class="post-header wrap-narrow">
  <span class="eyebrow">Columnar · Benchmarks</span>
  <h1><%= post["title"] %></h1>
  <p class="post-meta"><time datetime="<%= post["date"] %>"><%= post["display_date"] %></time> · <%= post["read_minutes"] %> min read</p>
  <p class="post-lede">Third of the series, after graphs and time series: a wide table of events and four analytical questions, against ClickHouse. SoliDB 2.10.4 answered each of them in 2.4 seconds on a million rows, whatever the question, where ClickHouse took one to ten milliseconds. Its columnar collections were columnar in name: one key per cell. This post is about what they are now, the figures of the build that came out of it, and what a table of four rows does not say about ClickHouse.</p>
  <p class="post-note">Every figure on this page was measured on November 15, 2026 on one machine, each database alone in a container with 4 cores and 16 GB, the harness on other cores. SoliDB is a build of the branch that follows 2.10.4, not a published release. The harness, the settings and the raw results are in the repository, under <code>benchmarks/compare</code> (<code>col.sh</code>, <code>results/2026-11-15-columnar</code>).</p>
</header>
<div class="wrap-narrow"><%- partial("blog/cover", {"post": post, "css_class": "post-cover", "loading": "eager"}) %></div>

<div class="post-body wrap-narrow">
<nav class="post-toc" aria-label="In this post">
  <p class="post-toc-title">In this post</p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#what">What was measured</a></li>
    <li><a href="#results">The results</a></li>
    <li><a href="#before">Where 2.10.4 stood</a></li>
    <li><a href="#blocks">What changed: rows in blocks</a></li>
    <li><a href="#fold">What changed: queries over arrays</a></li>
