  </ol>
</nav>

<section id="what"><h2>What was measured</h2>
<p>The table is generated: 10,000,000 events of the form <code>{ts, user, country, device, amount, bytes}</code>, among 100,000 users, 50 countries and 5 devices. Each row is a function of its number, so the harness computes every answer itself. The rows are loaded from eight connections, 20,000 at a time, as JSON.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>What one request asks</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>filter_agg</code></td><td>The count and the sum of the amounts of one country: 200,000 rows of the ten million.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>group_all</code></td><td>Per country, the count and the sum of bytes: every row, 50 groups.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>window_group</code></td><td>Per device, the count and the sum of bytes over a twentieth of the time span: 500,000 rows, 5 groups.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>top_users</code></td><td>The ten users with the largest total amount: every row, 100,000 groups.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>SoliDB receives SDBQL over its native driver, on a columnar collection. ClickHouse 26.10.4 receives SQL over HTTP, on a <code>MergeTree</code> table ordered by <code>(country, ts)</code>, with the two string columns as <code>LowCardinality</code>, and the table optimised to one part after the load. That order is the one that suits the first question: ClickHouse then reads the rows of one country and nothing else. Every answer is compared with the harness's own, before the timing and during it; no timed request returned a wrong answer on either.</p>
</section>

<section id="results"><h2>The results</h2>
<p>Requests per second, median of three runs of 20 seconds, with the median latency in parentheses:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Ten million rows</th><th>SoliDB</th><th>ClickHouse</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>filter_agg</code>, 1 client</td><td>721 (1.4&nbsp;ms)</td><td>562 (1.7&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>filter_agg</code>, 4 clients</td><td>2,683 (1.4&nbsp;ms)</td><td>1,307 (2.9&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>group_all</code>, 1 client</td><td>53.1 (18.0&nbsp;ms)</td><td>47.0 (20.8&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>group_all</code>, 4 clients</td><td>53.7 (38.0&nbsp;ms)</td><td>50.3 (78.8&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>window_group</code>, 1 client</td><td>189 (5.4&nbsp;ms)</td><td>120 (8.3&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>window_group</code>, 4 clients</td><td>334 (11.9&nbsp;ms)</td><td>193 (20.2&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>top_users</code>, 1 client</td><td>39.2 (25.5&nbsp;ms)</td><td>20.4 (48.6&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>top_users</code>, 4 clients</td><td>50.1 (93.7&nbsp;ms)</td><td>21.1 (187.3&nbsp;ms)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<figure class="post-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 160" role="img" aria-labelledby="col-top-t">
<title id="col-top-t">The ten users with the largest total among ten million rows, one client, requests per second: SoliDB 39.2, ClickHouse 20.4.</title>
<text x="20" y="30" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#9ba39c">Top ten of 100,000 users by total amount, one client (requests per second)</text>
<line x1="170" y1="46" x2="170" y2="120" stroke="#29322f"/>
<text x="20" y="72" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SoliDB</text>
<rect x="170" y="56" width="440" height="24" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/>
<text x="620" y="73" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f5a623">39.2</text>
<text x="20" y="110" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">ClickHouse</text>
<rect x="170" y="94" width="229" height="24" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/>
<text x="409" y="111" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">20.4</text>
<text x="20" y="146" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">Bars share one scale starting at 0. 10,000,000 rows, 4 cores each.</text>
</svg><figcaption>Every row is read and a hundred thousand groups are built. On 2.10.4 this question took 2.8 seconds on a tenth of the rows.</figcaption></figure>
<p>SoliDB has the higher figure in the eight rows, from 7% (<code>group_all</code> at four clients) to 2.4 times (<code>top_users</code> at four clients). <code>group_all</code> is close at both concurrencies: the two databases read ten million strings and add ten million integers, and neither has a shortcut. On the first question ClickHouse reads the rows of one country, which its table is ordered by, and SoliDB reads a list of them it keeps per block; on the last, both build a hundred thousand groups.</p>
<p>Loading the ten million rows: ClickHouse 7.2 seconds, SoliDB 7.0. The disk is not compared here: when the readings ended SoliDB's rows were still in its write buffer and its log, so the size of its directory said nothing of what the blocks take once flushed.</p>
</section>

<section id="before"><h2>Where 2.10.4 stood</h2>
<p>On a million rows, one client, the released 2.10.4 answered every one of the four questions 0.4 times a second: 2.4 seconds each, the filtered sum of one country like the top ten of a hundred thousand users. ClickHouse answered them 1,000, 417, 352 and 96 times a second. Ten million rows would not have finished a run.</p>
<p>The flat 2.4 seconds said what was wrong before the code did. A columnar collection stored each cell under its own key, <code>col:{column}:{row}</code>, as JSON text compressed on its own, and a full copy of each row beside. A query listed the rows, then read every cell it needed with one point read, decompressed it, parsed it, and built each row as an object before looking at the FILTER. Six columns and a million rows were six million point reads, whatever the question asked for.</p>
</section>

<section id="blocks"><h2>What changed: rows in blocks</h2>
<p>A row of a columnar collection is now in exactly one place:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A block</strong>: up to 65,536 rows in the order they were inserted, one array per column. An array is of the kind its values are: integers, floats, booleans, or strings as a sorted dictionary and one small number per row, each with a mask for nulls. A column whose values are of several kinds keeps each as JSON text. An insert of 4,096 rows or more becomes a block at once.</li>
<li><strong>The tail</strong>: rows that arrived a few at a time, one key each, sealed into a block when 4,096 of them wait.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing changed in how a collection is used: the same endpoints, the same driver commands, the same rows in the same order, the same replication. A collection written by an earlier version is converted the first time it is loaded: its rows are sealed into blocks and its cells deleted.</p>
<p>One thing the tests of the new storage found was older than it. A float inserted a few rows at a time went through JSON text, and came back with its last digit changed for some values: <code>93.33333333333333</code> returned as <code>93.33333333333331</code>. The tail is MessagePack now, which holds a float as it is.</p>
</section>

<section id="fold"><h2>What changed: queries over arrays</h2>
<p>Storing arrays is half of it. A query that builds a row object out of them to evaluate a FILTER gets little of the benefit: with the blocks alone, the four questions went from 0.4 to 1.7 requests a second. So a <code>COLLECT</code> over a columnar collection is now computed on the arrays, when the query is of the shape analytics has:</p>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> sdbql</div><pre><span class="tok-kw">FOR</span> r <span class="tok-kw">IN</span> events
  <span class="tok-kw">FILTER</span> r.ts &gt;= <span class="tok-bind">@from</span> <span class="tok-kw">AND</span> r.ts &lt; <span class="tok-bind">@to</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">COLLECT</span> device = r.device
  <span class="tok-kw">AGGREGATE</span> n = <span class="tok-fn">COUNT</span>(), bytes = <span class="tok-fn">SUM</span>(r.bytes)
  <span class="tok-kw">RETURN</span> { device, n, bytes }</pre></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>A comparison selects rows by reading one array.</strong> An equality on a string column is an equality on a dictionary code, and when it is the first condition the block already lists the rows of each string: nothing is scanned. That is <code>filter_agg</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Groups are a table filled on several threads</strong>, each thread taking its share of the blocks, and the few groups of each merged at the end. That is <code>group_all</code> and <code>window_group</code>.</li>
<li><strong>A key with many values is shared out differently.</strong> Merging a hundred thousand groups per thread cost more than computing them. For a single numeric key, each thread reads every block and keeps the rows of its own share of the keys: every group is then in one thread's table and nothing is merged. When the key is an integer of small range, the table is a plain array indexed by the key. That is <code>top_users</code>, which went from 3 requests a second to 226 on a million rows along the way.</li>
<li><strong>A <code>SORT … LIMIT</code> on an aggregate keeps only the groups that can be returned</strong>: those at or past the value of the last one, ties included, so that the sort which follows decides exactly as it did.</li>
</ul>
<p>As for time series, the constraint was that the answers do not change. SoliDB's <code>SUM</code> is exact and keeps integers as integers; the arrays feed the same accumulators as rows do, a group at a time, with the exact state of each sum. A block the arrays cannot answer for — a column with nulls, a comparison with a value of another kind — and the rows of the tail are read as objects and go through the ordinary path. The tests run each query both ways, on one thread and on several.</p>
<p>Any other query over a columnar collection — a <code>RETURN</code> of rows, an <code>OR</code>, a function in a key — still builds each row first. It is correct, and no faster than building rows from arrays can be.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits"><h2>What this does not show</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Four questions on one table.</strong> No join, no window function, no <code>DISTINCT</code> count, no string search, no hundred columns, no table larger than memory. ClickHouse is built for all of that; this comparison touches the corner where a document database with a columnar collection can reasonably be expected to stand.</li>
<li><strong>The blocks are kept in memory</strong>, all of them, once read, with the ids of their rows: about 1.4 GB of the server's memory for these ten million rows. There is no ceiling on it yet. ClickHouse reads what a query needs from disk and holds far less.</li>
<li><strong>ClickHouse ordered its table for the first question</strong>, and still reads more than SoliDB lists; ordered by time instead, it would do better on <code>window_group</code> and worse on <code>filter_agg</code>. We did not try other orders, nor projections, nor more than its defaults for threads and memory.</li>
<li><strong>The values are quarters of whole numbers</strong>, whose sums are exact in floating point, and SoliDB's plain-array path relies on sums that lost nothing. So the four questions were run again with amounts that are thirds, values that round and sums that round (one run of 10 seconds each): <code>filter_agg</code> 717 against 567 at one client, <code>top_users</code> 21.9 against 20.4 and 26.4 against 20.9 at four. The order holds, by less: a first version of that path gave up its plain sums as soon as one rounded, and answered <code>top_users</code> 4.9 times a second. It now keeps, beside each sum, the total of what its additions rounded away, which together are still the exact sum.</li>
<li><strong>The load is JSON over HTTP on both sides</strong>, which measures parsing as much as storage. ClickHouse's native formats load several times faster than this; SoliDB has no equivalent.</li>
<li><strong>DuckDB is not here.</strong> It was on the list. It runs inside the process that queries it, which is another comparison, and it is not written yet.</li>
<li><strong>This is a build of a branch.</strong> When it is released, the published image will be measured again and its results put beside these.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="run"><h2>Running it</h2>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> terminal</div><pre>cd benchmarks/compare
cargo build --release --bin solidb-col
./col.sh all                         <span class="tok-com"># SoliDB then ClickHouse, ten million rows</span>
ROWS=1000000 WARM=1 SECS=5 RUNS=1 ./col.sh all   <span class="tok-com"># a smaller pass</span></pre></div>
<p>The README of <code>benchmarks/compare</code> describes the table, the four scenarios, how each database is set up and what is checked; the exact statements are in the result files. If one of them is not the one you would have written for ClickHouse, that is the place to say so.</p>
</section>
</div>
